1
00:00:02,215 --> 00:00:04,383
So we got our first trie. We can insert

2
00:00:04,383 --> 00:00:07,418
data. Now let's see how we can find

3
00:00:07,578 --> 00:00:10,292
something. So I'll add a find method, and we

4
00:00:10,292 --> 00:00:13,037
want to find by key. That's the idea behind

5
00:00:13,037 --> 00:00:15,928
the trie. The keys are our identifiers. The

6
00:00:15,928 --> 00:00:18,705
keys are what allows us to find a value.

7
00:00:19,701 --> 00:00:21,114
So how do we find here?

8
00:00:22,013 --> 00:00:24,213
In the end, we can use a quite similar logic

9
00:00:24,422 --> 00:00:27,442
to inserting. We can again create a for loop

10
00:00:27,506 --> 00:00:30,877
where we start at 0 and keep on going

11
00:00:30,990 --> 00:00:32,254
through our key characters.

12
00:00:33,110 --> 00:00:34,882
So the same logic as we had it for

13
00:00:35,093 --> 00:00:35,548
inserting.

14
00:00:39,387 --> 00:00:40,344
And we can also again

15
00:00:40,928 --> 00:00:42,274
start at our root node

16
00:00:43,507 --> 00:00:45,939
and then change this node for every

17
00:00:45,972 --> 00:00:46,491
iteration.

18
00:00:49,083 --> 00:00:50,662
In the end here, we still also want to get

19
00:00:50,662 --> 00:00:51,626
that alphabet index.

20
00:00:52,486 --> 00:00:53,830
And of course, we therefore could also

21
00:00:53,927 --> 00:00:56,160
outsource this formula into a separate

22
00:00:56,451 --> 00:00:58,782
method, but I'll just copy it. So we get

23
00:00:58,782 --> 00:01:00,530
this alphabet index for our key here.

24
00:01:03,054 --> 00:01:03,926
And once we got that,

25
00:01:04,589 --> 00:01:05,009
we see

26
00:01:05,638 --> 00:01:07,625
if we can find a node for this given

27
00:01:07,884 --> 00:01:09,289
identified alphabet index.

28
00:01:10,468 --> 00:01:12,358
Because we don't necessarily need to have

29
00:01:12,407 --> 00:01:14,826
one if we're trying to find a key that

30
00:01:14,842 --> 00:01:17,115
hasn't been inserted before, we'll not be

31
00:01:17,278 --> 00:01:19,128
able to find all nodes along the way.

32
00:01:20,053 --> 00:01:21,383
So we simply

33
00:01:21,976 --> 00:01:23,562
reach out to our node and there to its

34
00:01:23,562 --> 00:01:24,027
children

35
00:01:24,604 --> 00:01:26,142
for the given alphabet index,

36
00:01:26,927 --> 00:01:29,507
and we check if we don't find this, if that

37
00:01:29,507 --> 00:01:32,680
is falsy, if we don't have a node for that

38
00:01:32,712 --> 00:01:33,001
given

39
00:01:33,914 --> 00:01:34,379
index.

40
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,766
And if that's the case, we simply return

41
00:01:37,263 --> 00:01:38,834
false or null,

42
00:01:39,747 --> 00:01:41,926
or you throw an error. That's totally up to

43
00:01:41,926 --> 00:01:42,039
you.

44
00:01:43,064 --> 00:01:45,115
If we make it past this if check though, we

45
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:47,789
we know that we did find a node, so now we

46
00:01:47,789 --> 00:01:50,285
can progress to the next character of our

47
00:01:50,285 --> 00:01:50,527
key.

48
00:01:51,090 --> 00:01:52,427
So we simply update node

49
00:01:53,168 --> 00:01:54,492
and set it equal to,

50
00:01:55,209 --> 00:01:57,672
well, the node which we just accessed on our

51
00:01:57,819 --> 00:01:58,634
children array.

52
00:02:00,553 --> 00:02:02,506
And once we're done with the for loop, we

53
00:02:02,716 --> 00:02:05,298
either return false in between if we didn't

54
00:02:05,298 --> 00:02:08,397
find some node, or if we really completed

55
00:02:08,397 --> 00:02:11,512
the for loop, we hopefully found a value.

56
00:02:12,364 --> 00:02:14,327
So now we can return

57
00:02:16,049 --> 00:02:16,677
our node

58
00:02:17,433 --> 00:02:20,185
which we have in the end because we updated

59
00:02:20,410 --> 00:02:22,522
in every iteration. So at the end we have

60
00:02:22,522 --> 00:02:24,957
the node with the value we were looking for.

61
00:02:26,070 --> 00:02:27,634
Of course you could also just return

62
00:02:27,827 --> 00:02:28,279
node.value.

63
00:02:29,076 --> 00:02:31,224
That really depends on what you want in your

64
00:02:31,272 --> 00:02:31,886
application.

65
00:02:32,887 --> 00:02:34,194
So let's give this a try now.

66
00:02:36,010 --> 00:02:38,611
Here I log the entire trie, but thereafter I

67
00:02:38,676 --> 00:02:39,742
want to console.log

68
00:02:40,336 --> 00:02:41,926
try.find

69
00:02:42,504 --> 00:02:44,141
for, let's say, age,

70
00:02:44,928 --> 00:02:45,057
which

71
00:02:45,715 --> 00:02:46,614
should work of course,

72
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:47,706
also

73
00:02:48,685 --> 00:02:50,515
for names and for name,

74
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:53,774
but also for, let's say, hobby, which

75
00:02:53,903 --> 00:02:55,219
clearly does not exist.

76
00:02:56,247 --> 00:02:57,660
And if we now save all that

77
00:02:58,334 --> 00:02:58,992
and reload,

78
00:02:59,763 --> 00:03:01,577
we see we find the nodes for

79
00:03:02,171 --> 00:03:02,637
the age,

80
00:03:03,311 --> 00:03:03,504
for

81
00:03:04,339 --> 00:03:06,458
the, the names, and for the single name But

82
00:03:06,458 --> 00:03:08,624
we also get false at the end, which of

83
00:03:08,673 --> 00:03:10,374
course is the console.log statement for

84
00:03:10,374 --> 00:03:13,519
hobby because that is not existing in our

85
00:03:13,583 --> 00:03:13,840
trie.

86
00:03:14,707 --> 00:03:17,178
And that is a very basic yet fully

87
00:03:17,258 --> 00:03:19,650
functional trie which we can build and which

88
00:03:19,762 --> 00:03:21,832
works exactly as a trie should work.
